      "name": null,
      "problem": "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}$ defined for $x\\in (0,\\infty )$ have a local maximum?",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot {x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot 2x}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ on $(0,\\infty )$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{3}} & =0 \\\\ 1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & ={e}^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x={e}^{1/2}$ is a stationary point. Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x={e}^{1/3},e$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}({e}^{1/3}) & =\\frac{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{1/3}}{{({e}^{1/3})}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{3e}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(e) & =\\frac{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e}{{e}^{3}}=-\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,{e}^{1/2})$ | ${e}^{1/2}$ | $({e}^{1/2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | ${e}^{1/3}$ |  | $e$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\n- At $x={e}^{1/2},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x={e}^{1/2}$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x={e}^{1/2}.$",
