      "name": null,
      "problem": "Given the function $f(x)=-{x}^{5},$ which of the following statements are true?",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-5{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-5{x}^{4}=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nSo $x=0$ is the only stationary point of $f(x).$ Next, to determine whether this point is local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =-5(-1)^{4}=-5<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =-5(1)^{4}=-5<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ |  | $↘$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=0.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=0$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nHence, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema.",
